## 📘 Phase 3 — Use Case Catalog

> For each FC, produce a full Use Case card with all observable behavioral attributes.

### 3.1 — Use Case Card Construction

```
FOR EACH FC-ID, create a UC card with the following attributes.
ALL values derived from code — not invented:

  UC-ID:
    UC-[N] matching FC-[N]
    (If one FC has multiple distinct behavioral variants — e.g., different
    action types handled by same endpoint — create UC-[N]a, UC-[N]b, etc.)

  Actor:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - OpenAPI spec consumer description (if present)
      - Controller class name prefix (e.g., ExternalApiController → "External System")
      - @CrossOrigin origin values (if present)
      - Swagger @Tag annotations
      - Integration test setup: what test actor is simulated?
      - Kafka consumer group name (e.g., "billing-service" → "Billing Service")
      - README "Used By" or "Consumers" section
      - If undetermined: mark as "Unknown — see README"

  Precondition:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - @Valid annotations on request body → what must be structurally valid
      - Guard clauses at method entry (if (x == null) throw ...)
      - @PreAuthorize / security annotations → auth requirement
      - Required request parameters (@RequestParam required=true)
      - Kafka consumer: what state must exist before processing
      - Any explicit validation at entry point before main logic runs

  Flow:
    TRACE THE EXECUTION PATH:
      Step 1: Entry (controller/handler receives request)
      Step 2: Input validation layer (what validators run first?)
      Step 3: Business logic layer (what service methods are called?)
      Step 4: Data access (what repositories, caches, external calls happen?)
      Step 5: Response construction (what is returned?)

    REPRESENT AS:
      "[Entry] → [Validation Layer] → [Business Logic] → [Data Access] → [Response]"
      Use actual class/method names, not generic labels.
      Use + to show parallel steps, → for sequential steps.

  Postcondition:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - Return type of handler method
      - HTTP status codes set (@ResponseStatus, ResponseEntity<>)
      - What state changes are made (DB write, cache update, event published)
      - What the test assertions verify (look at test assertThat/assertEquals)

  Error Paths:
    DERIVE FROM:
      - catch blocks in controller and service layers
      - @ExceptionHandler methods in @ControllerAdvice classes
      - Custom exception classes and their messages
      - Validation error response patterns
      - Test cases that test failure scenarios
      - Error code constants / enums
    FORMAT:
      "[Trigger condition] → [Error code or exception] → [HTTP status or behavior]"

  Key Difference (only for variant UCs):
    What distinguishes THIS variant from other similar UCs?
    Derive from: conditional logic branches, different request fields required,
    different validator chains invoked, different downstream calls made.
```

